{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"reasoning","text":"Main observations:\n\n1. **Query**: 42 guide groups — 10 NT groups (NT-I1..NT-I10, ~1534 cells total) + 32 perturbation guides (guide1..guide32) of varying sizes (208 down to 3 cells).\n\n2. **Reference**: 43 guide groups — 10 NT groups + 33 perturbation guides targeting 33 genes (34 target_gene categories including NT). This is a Perturb-seq dataset targeting RNA processing factors (CPSF, CSTF, SCAF, PAF complex, etc.).\n\n3. var indices are not identical (`all var equal: False`) — need to intersect/align genes. Both have 11889 genes. Order may be different or some genes may differ. Need to align to common genes.\n\n4. Raw counts (integers, library sizes vary).\n\nSo mapping task: map the query's 32 perturbation guides (+ NT) to the reference's 33 target genes (+ NT). There are 32 query guides and 33 reference genes, so one reference gene may have no corresponding query counterpart (or one query guide maps to NT... unlikely). Query NT groups correspond to reference NT.\n\nCell type shift: need to check what this means. Let me do a simple PCA/UMAP to see the relationship between query and reference cell types. The query might be a different cell line (e.g., a different clone) or a shifted composition.\n\nApproach (main method): **Correlation of perturbation signatures**.\n- Align genes to common var.\n- Normalize: log1p CPM per cell.\n- Compute pseudobulk signatures: for each guide group, take the mean of log1p-CPM across cells, subtract the mean of NT within the dataset → perturbation effect vector (over genes).\n- For the reference, aggregate guide-level signatures into gene-level signatures by averaging the signatures of that gene's guides (here each gene has exactly one guide, so trivially identical).\n- Score each query perturbation guide against each reference target gene using Pearson correlation across genes.\n- score = best r, runner_up_score = second-best r (among genes), confidence = margin = score − runner_up_score.\n- Quantifying ambiguity: margin distribution; plus empirical null: permutation by shuffling the gene-axis labels of the query signature to obtain the null maximum correlation → p-value per guide; additionally report rank gap and count of near-ties (Δr < 0.01).\n\nValidation (single run):\n- Self-mapping control: map each reference guide against reference gene signatures excluding itself → verify that the method can recover the true gene at high accuracy, establish expected score/margin scale under no shift.\n- Cross-dataset positive control: query NT vs reference NT should correlate well with each other (baseline sanity check).\n- Independent second method for validation: per-cell nearest-centroid classifier using reference gene-level centroids of residuals... Simpler: train a multinomial classifier on reference perturbation cells (log1p CPM, maybe with cell mean regressed out) and classify query cells, aggregate votes per guide. But cell type shift degrades raw classifiers. Alternative: use signature-based second method: Spearman instead of Pearson — still a different method. Or per-cell mapping via correlation with reference gene centroids in PCA space. Let me do this: per-cell voting with reference gene centroids computed in a shared low-dim PC space, where PCs are fit on combined data or on reference only; project query cells; nearest centroid in PC space weighted by... Actually, cleaner independent check: for each query guide, take the mean of per-cell top-1 assignments (nearest reference gene signature centroid on signatures restricted to... hmm).\n\nSimplest robust validation: leave-one-out self-mapping on reference (establishes that the pipeline works), and Spearman as the alternative metric for the query mapping (robustness check, cheap). Additionally, report the NT↔NT correlation as a positive control for the shift. This fits \"one primary method + one lightweight validation path.\"\n\nWait — \"cell type shift\" — let me actually check what shift exists. Query NT groups are labeled NT-I1..I10, same naming style as reference. Query X row sums vs reference — similar magnitudes. Cell type shift may be subtle (e.g., query is a different cell cycle composition, or different cell line K562 vs something). Reference may be a different cell line. Let me quickly visualize with PCA to characterize the shift for the report. This is cheap (one run).\n\nAlso \"prevent target metadata leakage\": in analysis.py we must ensure:\n- Mapping uses only expression matrices and group identity (guide labels as group keys only), not gene names for the query.\n- Reference target_gene is used only to aggregate reference signatures and for final reporting.\n- Explicit guard: assert that no query metadata contains gene symbols; the script should not use string matching between query guide IDs and reference gene names (e.g., 'guide1' contains no gene information).\n- In self-mapping validation, exclude the guide being mapped from its own signature computation (leave-one-out) to avoid trivial self-matching.\n\nNow, deliverable CSV: target_gene, query_guide_id, score, runner_up_score, confidence. Rows: one row per query guide. Include NT rows? The task says \"map the query's perturbation groups across cell type shift to the labeled reference, and identify query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1.\" I think it's reasonable to include NT as a mapped group (target_gene=NT) — that's part of the mapping, and its score/runner_up can be defined as correlation against reference gene signatures (best non-NT gene should be low). Hmm, but score semantics for NT: correlate query NT pseudobulk (vs query NT baseline → residual of NT against itself is ~0) against reference signatures... Query NT signature is computed relative to query NT mean, so query NT signature = 0 vector → correlation undefined. Better: for NT rows, either define score as correlation of query NT mean profile against reference NT mean profile (sanity) with runner_up against best perturbation gene, or simply mark NT rows with score = correlation of query NT pseudobulk against reference NT pseudobulk. Simpler and cleaner: include NT rows, with score = Pearson r between query NT mean profile and reference NT mean profile, runner_up_score = max r against any perturbation gene signature (computed on raw profiles? inconsistent).\n\nAlternative cleaner approach: exclude NT from the CSV's perturbation mapping, mention NT mapping in the report. But graders often check the full mapping table including controls. Hmm. Task statement: \"map the query's perturbation groups to the labeled reference\" — perturbation groups = the 32 guide groups. NT is a control group, not a perturbation group. Let's make the CSV 32 rows for perturbation guides and document the NT↔NT mapping in the report (with its profile correlation as a sanity number). Actually, to be safe, include the NT row with target_gene=\"NT\" and score computed as profile-level Pearson r(query NT mean, ref NT mean), runner_up_score = max over perturbation genes of profile-level r(query NT mean, ref gene mean)? These raw profile correlations are dominated by cell type, not perturbation. Inconsistent but honest; will document. Hmm, I want to keep the CSV semantics clean: all rows scored by the same signature correlation method. For NT, the signature against the query's own NT baseline is degenerate.\n\nDecision: CSV = 32 perturbation guide rows only (target_gene among the 33 reference genes). Report mentions NT↔NT correspondence separately along with the profile correlation sanity check. That's the cleanest interpretation of \"perturbation groups.\"\n\nNext, the cell type shift question. Let me quickly verify: PCA on combined data, color by dataset and guide class. This will inform whether we should regress something out. Let me run a simple exploration once."}}
