{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"reasoning","text":"Next, I'll write analysis.py. Let's be careful about the design:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nMap the query Perturb-seq perturbation groups to the labeled reference\nacross cell-type shifts, and identify the query guide IDs for PABPC1,\nNUDT21, and LEO1.\n\nMethod (primary method):\n  - log1p CPM normalization on common genes.\n  - Per-dataset NT (non-targeting) baseline = mean of log1p-CPM over all NT-* cells.\n  - Perturbation signature per guide group = group mean − NT baseline (same dataset).\n  - Reference signatures are aggregated at the target_gene level.\n  - Features: top-K genes ranked by variance across reference gene-level signatures\n    (feature selection is supervised only from the labeled reference; nothing from query labels).\n  - Score(query guide, reference gene) = Pearson r between signatures on the K features.\n  - score = max r, runner_up_score = 2nd best r, confidence = margin.\n\nLeakage prevention:\n  - Query obs: only the `guide` column is read and used only as the group key.\n  - Assert that the query has no target_gene column, and that query guide IDs do\n    not lexically match reference gene names (no string-match shortcut).\n  - Reference target_gene is used only for reference signature aggregation and final reporting.\n  - No joint embedding/clustering is used for label transfer.\n\nAmbiguity quantification:\n  - Margin (score − runner_up), empirical gene-axis permutation p-value\n    (B=200, max-r null), near-tie count (Δr<0.02), low n cell flag.\n\nValidation (lightweight, 1 pass):\n  - Subsample stability: recompute signatures on 80% of cells per group,\n    compare top-1 retention.\n  - Metric agreement with Spearman on the same features.\n  - NT vs NT raw-profile correlation sanity check + raw-profile centroid control\n    (expected to fail due to cell-type shift, demonstrating the need for NT reference).\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Load the h5ads and intersect the genes.\n- Normalize: sc.pp.normalize_total(target_sum=1e4); log1p. Use scanpy on in-memory AnnData (small scale).\n- NT detection: guide labels starting with \"NT\".\n- Baseline: mean of NT cells per dataset (vector over genes).\n- Signature: mean per group − baseline. NT group excluded from mapping targets.\n- Reference gene signatures: group reference guides by target_gene; average guide signatures (equal weight per guide).\n- Feature selection: variance of reference gene signatures across genes, top K=1500.\n- Correlation helper: vectorized Pearson (z-scored vectors, dot product).\n- Permutation: for each query guide, permute feature indices B times (permutation over genes within the K-feature subspace — permute the query signature's entries across the K features), compute max |r|? Use max r (positive). p = (1 + #{null max_r >= score}) / (1 + B).\n  Actually, to make the null more meaningful, permute over the full gene set (11858) and then restrict to K features — equivalent to permutation within the K features. Permute within the K features.\n- Validation subsample: seed 0, sample 80% of cells from each group (both datasets' NT + all guide groups), recompute everything with the same features (recompute features from the reference subsample to keep the pipeline honest), get top-1 gene per query guide, retention rate %.\n- Spearman variant: rank-transform the signatures within each vector and use Pearson → top-1 agreement with primary method.\n- Raw-profile centroid control: mean raw log1p profile per reference gene (including its NT) + reference NT; assign each query guide by max Pearson to reference gene raw-profile centroids → report accuracy... query has no labels, so we can't compute accuracy; instead, report the mean correlation of query guide raw profiles with reference NT centroid vs the correlation with the matched reference gene signature — demonstrate dataset shift: corr(query guide raw, ref NT raw profile) >> corr with any reference gene raw centroid? Hmm, reference gene raw centroids are dominated by the reference baseline. A simple demonstration: Pearson r between query NT mean raw profile and reference NT mean raw profile (should be high, e.g., >0.9 — same lineage), and the PC1 separation statistic. Then, centroid mapping of raw profiles: for each query guide, argmax over reference gene raw centroids — expect almost everything to have low correlation and dominated by dataset mean; report the distribution of those max r values compared to the signature score. This is descriptive.\n\nDeliverable CSV: 32 rows, columns exactly: target_gene, query_guide_id, score, runner_up_score, confidence. Sort by query_guide_id natural order (guide1..guide32)? Or by confidence? Sort by guide number for determinism. Round scores to 4 decimals.\n\ndiagnostics.json: full per-guide info (best gene, runner-up gene, r against all genes? Maybe top-5 list, p-value, n_cells, flags), plus global statistics (PC1 separation, NT-NT r, stability %, spearman agreement, feature K, number of common genes), plus the answer for genes of interest.\n\nAlso, output the mapping of PABPC1/NUDT21/LEO1.\n\nOne decision: K=1500 features. With 33 reference signatures, top 1500 genes by signature variance is reasonable (each signature is averaged over ~20–300 cells; the signatures of the smallest reference guides (SYMPK 8 cells, RPAP2 5 cells) are noisy — the variance-based feature selection might pick up noise genes from these. Maybe we should restrict signature variance computation to reference genes with n_cells >= 20? Or downweight. Compute feature variance using only reference gene signatures from guides with >= 20 cells (31 out of 33 genes), to avoid noise genes dominating. Document this.)\n\nWait, actually, there's a subtle point about mapping to reference genes with few cells (SYMPK n=8, RPAP2 n=5, SF3A1 n=12, THOC5 n=21): their signatures are noisy, so query guides that truly target those genes may have low scores. Nothing we can do about it; the ambiguity quantification will capture this.\n\nAlso the query has 32 guides and the reference has 33 genes → probably one reference gene is absent from the query. Fine.\n\nPermutation p-value cost: 32 guides × 200 permutations × 33 genes × K=1500 dot products: vectorize per guide: permuted signature matrix (B × K) @ reference signature matrix (K × 33) → (B × 33) per guide, after z-scoring. Total flops ~ 32*200*1500*33 ≈ 3.2e8 — fine with numpy.\n\nNow let's write the file. Create the output directory. Write the script in a single here-string. Be careful with PowerShell here-strings and quoting: use @\" ... \"@ and avoid `${}` interpolation issues — PowerShell only interpolates $ variables inside a double-quoted here-string. My Python code may contain `$` (rare) — avoid `$` characters in the code. Use @'...'@ (single-quoted here-string, no interpolation) for safety. Single-quoted here-string: @' ... '@ — no variable expansion, safe for Python code containing backslashes and quotes.\n\nNow let's write analysis.py."}}
