{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"reasoning","text":"The pipeline ran to completion. Results:\n- PABPC1 → guide18 (margin 0.2416, strong)\n- NUDT21 → guide13 (margin 0.1112, strong)\n- LEO1 → no query guide assigned!\n\nLet me scrutinize this. Several concerning signs:\n\n1. LEO1 is unmapped: which query guide is LEO1's runner-up? Need to check LEO1's score in the diagnostics top-5 list.\n\n2. RPRD1B is hit 6 times (guide10, 16, 23, 28, 30, 32) — suspicious. Reference RPRD1B signature might be a \"generic\" attractor, or there might be query guides that truly map to RPRD1B, but 6 out of 32 is too many. Also CPSF2×3, CPSF6×3, SCAF1×3, CPSF4×2, PCF11×2. Query has 32 guides, reference has 33 genes, and if it's 1-to-1, then each reference gene should be hit roughly once. Multiple hits on the same gene suggest either: (a) some query guides are unmappable/noisy and land on an attractor, or (b) the query truly has multiple guides per gene (design: reference has 1 guide per gene but query may have multiple guides per gene — reference guide names have -I1..-I8 suffixes indicating independent guides; the query's anonymized guides could include multiple per gene). Hmm. Query has 32 perturbation guides; reference has 33 genes. If query is a 1-to-1 screen minus one gene, then duplicates are erroneous mappings.\n\n3. The margins are generally small (many <0.05). Spearman agreement is only 81%. Subsample stability is 93.8%.\n\nThere might be a better approach. Let me think about what's limiting performance:\n\n- Signatures are mean differences; Pearson correlation over 1500 features with modest cell counts (some guides have n<50) is noisy.\n- Cell-type shift may interact with perturbation effects: perturbation signatures may partially differ between cell types; conserved core signatures dominate for strong perturbations.\n\nImprovements to consider (methodologically sound, still one main method):\n1. **Better normalization against shift**: the query's PC1 has