{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"reasoning","text":"Very informative. Key findings:\n\n1. **LEO1's pull is weak**: the maximum r for LEO1 across all query guides is only 0.353 (guide14). By comparison, strong matches are 0.66–0.73. guide14's own top hits are PAF1 (0.455) > CTR9 (0.440) > CDC73 (0.428) > SCAF1 > LEO1 (0.353). So guide14 is most likely targeting a subunit of the PAF complex — and among the four, LEO1 ranks last within the complex for guide14.\n\n2. guide32 (3 cells) is pure noise (all r ≈ 0.07).\n\n3. Many weak guides (guide1 with n=199 has a max r of only 0.221! guide10 n=16 max 0.199; guide23 n=83 max 0.368). These might be: failed perturbations (no transcriptomic effect), guides targeting genes not in the reference, or weak signatures due to cell-type shift. Notably, guide1 has 199 cells but a max r of only 0.221 — despite the large n, the signature doesn't match anything in the reference well. Hmm, guide1 might be targeting a gene not in the reference, or might be a perturbation with a weak effect. Similarly guide7 (n=208) → PAPOLA r=0.473 — moderate.\n\nWait — another important observation: for CPSF4/CPSF3/CPSF2/CSTF3/CSTF1/SYMPK/CPSF7, **guide4 is the \"best_guide\"**: the pull of many CPSF/CSTF genes peaks at guide4 (r 0.63–0.71, with CPSF1 highest at 0.725). The greedy method assigned guide4→CPSF1. This is consistent with guide4 being CPSF1 (its perturbation signature resembles other CPSF-family knockdowns because the complex is destabilized — a common phenomenon: perturbing one subunit destabilizes the whole complex, producing similar signatures). This is the classic Perturb-seq \"complex-level\" confounding: CPSF1 KD phenocopies CPSF2/3/4 KD. Therefore, discrimination within a complex is genuinely hard, and the reference signatures for complex members are highly correlated with each other.\n\n4. Under the 1:1 prior, Hungarian matching (npc=0, F features) gives a complete assignment: PAF-complex genes → guide10 (CDC73), guide14 (CTR9), guide28 (PAF1), guide32 (LEO1). But guide32 is noise, guide28 is ambiguous (top r 0.426–0.428 across 3 genes), guide10 is very weak (max 0.199). Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the query doesn't contain all 33 genes. If the query has 32 guides, and some query guides target genes not in the reference (decoys), or some reference genes are missing. The task explicitly asks to identify query guide IDs corresponding to PABPC1, NUDT21, LEO1 — implying all three exist in the query. So LEO1 must have a query guide. The candidates are guide14 (r=0.353, but PAF complex) or some noisy guide forced by exclusion.\n\nHmm, wait — let me reconsider. Among the noisy/weak guides (guide1, guide10, guide23, guide25..27, guide29..32), is there one where LEO1 ranks #1 or #2? In the LEO1 column, guide14 is highest at 0.353, and the next best is guide20 at 0.186. guide20's top hit is PCF11 (0.599 in the original run; here FIP1L1 is also pulled 0.568, THOC5 0.375, SF3A1 0.362). So the only meaningful LEO1 signal is guide14.\n\nTherefore, the most likely scenario is: guide14 targets one of the PAF complex genes {PAF1, CTR9, CDC73, LEO1}. Which one? On F features, PAF1 ranks first for guide14. But wait — what did the greedy F run assign for the PAF genes? Greedy: guide14→PAF1; guide28→? (greedy guide28→RPRD1B, r=0.428 vs PAF1 0.376); guide10→SYMPK (0.199); guide32→CPSF2 (0.075). So greedily, PAF-complex genes get matched as: PAF1←guide14; CTR9/CDC73/LEO1 unmatched (best remaining guides are weak).\n\nThe Hungarian solution distributes the four PAF genes across guides 10/14/28/32. Given that guide32 is pure noise (n=3, max r 0.075) and guide10 is very weak (max r 0.199), those assignments are unreliable.\n\nLet me look at guide28 more carefully (n=16): top r's are all around 0.38–0.43 (RPRD1B 0.428, SCAF1 0.427, CSTF1 0.426, SYMPK 0.385, PAF1 0.376, FIP1L1 0.363) — a flat, ambiguous profile.\n\nAn important consideration: maybe the query's true LEO1 guide has a weak signature because LEO1 KD has a mild effect, and/or because of cell-type shift. In the reference, LEO1-I2 has 246 cells — a well-measured signature. Across cell types, LEO1's effect may partially transfer.\n\nWhich gene among the four does guide14 most likely target? Let me check how correlated the reference PAF-complex signatures are with each other — if r(PAF1_sig, CTR9_sig) ≈ 0.9, then it's nearly impossible to distinguish them from the query side. If the grader's ground truth is 1:1, then the \"correct\" answer for LEO1 is whichever query guide truly targeted LEO1. Our best statistical evidence: guide14 has the strongest LEO1 affinity of any guide, but within guide14, LEO1 ranks 5th.\n\nHmm, but wait — I should also consider the possibility that one of the other weak guides is LEO1 and guide14 is, say, CTR9. Let me quantify with a proper probabilistic model: for each query guide g and gene t, compute likelihood p(g|t) using correlation under a noise model and assign via Hungarian on log-likelihood. But flat noise profiles will match anything.\n\nAlternative additional evidence: **second-best statistics and exclusion logic**. If we assume 1:1, then: strong, confident anchors claim genes first (guide4→CPSF1 r=0.725, guide13→NUDT21 0.708, guide18→PABPC1 0.664, guide20→PCF11 0.673, guide19→CPSF6 0.613, guide9→RBBP6 0.562, guide16→SCAF1 0.555, guide24→PABPN1 0.532, guide7→PAPOLA 0.473, guide6→SCAF4 0.403, guide2→CPSF7 0.293?, guide30→RPRD1B 0.473). Then the remaining genes get matched to the remaining guides. Hungarian does exactly this globally.\n\nThe Hungarian (F features) assignment had 18 differences from the greedy method. Its weakest links: guide32→LEO1 (n=3 noise), guide10→CDC73, guide28→PAF1, guide14→CTR9. Alternatively, a variant with PC regression (degraded) gave guide14→LEO1.\n\nNow, what about the alternative that guide14→LEO1, and CTR9/PAF1/CDC73 get assigned to guide10/guide28/guide32? For guide14, PAF1-CTR9-CDC73-LEO1 r's are 0.455/0.440/0.428/0.353 — the LEO1