same screen — e.g., same guide library, but the query was screened in a different cell type and some perturbations have weaker/no effects (cell-type-specific effects!). This is a hallmark of this kind of benchmark: perturbations like LEO1 may have weak effects in the query cell type. Then the query LEO1 guide could be any guide with a weak signature — including guide14 if LEO1 KD weakly phenocopies PAF-complex perturbation.\n\nLet me use another discriminator: within the PAF complex, which reference gene's signature best explains guide14 when accounting for the complex's shared component? Compute partial correlation: regress the mean PAF-complex signature out of both guide14 and each PAF gene signature, then correlate the residuals. The shared complex effect gets removed and gene-specific residual effects remain.\n\nAlso try: restrict features to those that discriminate within the PAF complex (genes with high variance across the 4 PAF signatures, low variance in NT). Then correlate guide14 with the 4 PAF signatures on those within-complex features. This is a targeted, legitimate discriminative analysis (reference labels only).\n\nLet me test that."}}
