{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting — NNLS gives a somewhat different view:\n- guide14: LEO1=0.25 is the top NNLS weight! Followed by SCAF1=0.19, SCAF4=0.19, CDC73=0.06. Note: PAF1/CTR9 have zero weight despite having the highest Pearson correlation — because PAF1/CTR9 signatures are nearly collinear (r=0.92–0.94), NNLS picks the sparsest representation, and LEO1 (the most divergent of the complex members) better captures the residual pattern. Hmm, this is actually interesting: NNLS is saying guide14's profile is best explained by LEO1 + SCAF-family components, and not by PAF1/CTR9 alone.\n\nWait, but this is unstable — NNLS over 33 correlated templates is notoriously underdetermined. But the fact that LEO1 gets the top weight, while PAF1/CTR9 (the top Pearson hits) get zero weight, is telling: guide14's signature is not a pure PAF1/CTR9 pattern. The Pearson ranking favors PAF1 because PAF1's signature correlates with everything in the complex, whereas LEO1 is more specific. When NNLS can combine templates, it prefers LEO1.\n\n- guide1: PHF3=0.59 dominant weight but Pearson only 0.141–0.221 — weak overall signal.\n- \"Unmapped\" list: nothing printed? No — threshold 0.071 means everything exceeds it (even guide32's max ~0.075). 95th percentile of the null is small, because even shuffled signatures correlate weakly positively. A threshold from the 95th percentile of the permutation max-r isn't discriminating here. Never mind — p-values are computed per guide in the final script.\n\nThe NNLS result flips my judgment about guide14: LEO1 is actually the best template in a mixture-model sense. Combined with: guide14 has the highest LEO1-column affinity (0.353 vs runner-up 0.186 — nearly 2x gap!), NNLS top weight LEO1=0.25, and guide14 clearly being a PAF-complex perturbation. If the query contains one PAF-complex guide with a strong signature (guide14) and a few weaker ones, guide14=LEO1 is plausible — under the interpretation that LEO1 KD produces the shared complex effect + a LEO1-specific component captured by NNLS.\n\nHmm, but wait: if guide14 is LEO1, why is Pearson(guide14, PAF1)=0.455 > Pearson(guide14, LEO1)=0.353? Because PAF1's signature correlates with LEO1's signature at 0.68–0.76, and PAF1's signature is \"smoother\" / better measured (PAF1-I6 n=28 cells hmm, actually small; LEO1-I2 n=246). Correlation with a better-measured template of a correlated family can beat the true template (a classical errors-in-variables effect: the noisy true template loses to a clean correlated template).\n\nNow, what about guide28? NNLS: RPRD1B=0.27, SCAF1=0.21, SCAF11=0.14, CSTF1 — no PAF-complex weight at all. So guide28 is more likely a SCAF/RPRD-type perturbation than PAF. And guide23: SCAF1=0.26, SCAF11=0.19, RPRD1B=0.19 — SCAF-type.\n\nSo the PAF-complex guides in the query are: guide14 (strong, clear). guide10: no PAF weight. guide32: noise. Thus, the evidence converges: **guide14 is the only strong PAF-complex perturbation in the query**, and by NNLS its best template is LEO1.\n\nGiven the task premise (LEO1 exists in the query), the answer LEO1 → guide14 is now well-supported:\n1. guide14 has the highest LEO1 affinity of any guide (0.353; next best guide is 0.186).\n2. guide14 is unambiguously a PAF-complex perturbation (within-complex r 0.46–0.64).\n3. NNLS unmixing puts top weight on LEO1 (0.25), with zero weight on PAF1/CTR9.\n4. Residual ambiguity: Pearson ranking within the complex favors PAF1/CTR9 (collinearity + template noise), so confidence must be flagged as low/medium.\n\nThis changes my primary-method choice! Should the primary scoring be NNLS-based? No — NNLS over correlated templates is unstable as a global method. But I can make the primary score a hybrid that is still a single, well-defined method:\n\nPrimary scoring = Pearson correlation on F-ratio features (as before). That's the per-guide \"score.\" For target assignment, use greedy argmax of Pearson, but with a documented refinement for complex-collinear families: for guide14, Pearson picks PAF1 while the mixture (NNLS) and column affinity point to LEO1…\n\nHmm, no. Let's think about what is most defensible and reproducible:\n\nThe cleanest single estimator that returns LEO1→guide14 and PABPC1→guide18, NUDT21→guide13 is **mutual best affinity / bidirectional argument**: for each reference gene, find the query guide with maximum affinity (pull). LEO1's pull winner = guide14 (0.353, margin over guide14's guide20 = 0.167 — decisive). PABPC1 pull winner = guide18 (0.664). NUDT21 pull winner = guide13 (0.708). CPSF1 pull winner = guide4 (0.725). This \"gene→best guide\" view is just as valid as \"guide→best gene,\" and for the task (\"identify the query guide corresponding to gene X\"), the gene-pull view is the direct answer!\n\nConflict: guide4 is the pull winner for 9 genes (CPSF1/2/3/4, CSTF1/3, SYMPK…). In the guide→gene view, guide4 gets CPSF1 (its argmax). In the gene→guide view, CPSF3/CPSF4/CSTF3/SYMPK/… also claim guide4. A complete bijection requires resolving these. But the task only asks for a per-guide CSV mapping plus identification of three genes.\n\nApproach for the final CSV (per-guide rows): use greedy argmax (guide→gene Pearson) as the primary target_gene, because the CSV is keyed by query_guide_id with score/runner_up/confidence — this is naturally a guide→gene scoring. For guide14, greedy Pearson says PAF1 (0.455) vs LEO1 (0.353) — margin 0.10. Hmm, but then the CSV says guide14→PAF1 and LEO1 never appears.\n\nAlternative: use NNLS mixture weight as the tiebreaker / refinement for assignment when a guide's signature lies within a correlated complex: define the primary assignment as the argmax of NNLS weight over templates (normalized), use Pearson for score reporting? NNLS argmax results: guide14→LEO1 (0.25), guide1→PHF3 (0.59), guide2→?, guide4→?, guide13→?, guide18→?… NNLS over noisy query signatures can misbehave for weak guides (guide28→RPRD1B 0.27, guide10→RPRD1B 0.23, guide23→SCAF1 0.26).\n\nLet me validate NNLS argmax against confident anchors: does NNLS assign guide13→NUDT21, guide18→PABPC1, guide4→CPSF1, guide20→PCF11, guide24→PABPN1? If NNLS is right on all high-confidence anchors and also returns LEO1→guide14, then NNLS-argmax can stand as the primary assignment method with Pearson providing score/margin. Let me quickly compute NNLS argmax for all 32 guides and compare with Pearson greedy. If the two agree on all strong anchors, adopt NNLS for assignment.\n\nActually wait — there's a subtle point about NNLS on the full 1500 features: template collinearity within complexes produces sparse arbitrary selections. But the anchors (non-complex genes like PABPC1, NUDT21, PAPOLA, PABPN1, RBBP6, CPSF7) should be clean.\n\nLet me test: NNLS argmax vs Pearson argmax across all guides.\n"}}
