{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"reasoning","text":"The pipeline ran to completion. Final answers:\n- PABPC1 → guide18 (score 0.6605, margin 0.2173)\n- NUDT21 → guide13 (score 0.7107, margin 0.1029)\n- LEO1 → guide14 (score 0.3349, margin −0.0844 — flagged as ambiguous, backed by mixture evidence)\n\nVery interesting new results:\n1. **Forced bijection: guide14=LEO1 total (13.366) ≥ guide14=PAF1 (13.344), and matches the unconstrained optimum (13.366)**! In other words, the globally optimal 1:1 assignment actually has guide14=LEO1 — because after guide14→LEO1, PAF1 can be placed elsewhere at nearly the same total score. This is strong, independent, quantitative support: the optimal bijection prefers guide14=LEO1. Let me check what the unconstrained Hungarian method assigned to PAF1/CTR9/CDC73… The unconstrained optimum equals the guide14=LEO1 constraint (13.366), meaning there exists an unconstrained optimal solution with guide14→LEO1.\n\n2. Per-cell vote: PAF1 0.327 > CTR9 0.255 > CDC73 0.236 > LEO1 0.182 — votes favor PAF1 (consistent with Pearson), but mean per-cell r values are all ~0.10-0.12 (barely above noise). So cell-level evidence is weak/ambiguous; mixture + bijection support LEO1.\n\n3. Subsample stability of the FINAL assignment is 81.2% (dropped from stage-1's 93.8%? Actually the previous 93.8% was stage-1 under the old variance features; this time final rule (including stage-2 flips that can fluctuate on subsample) under F features). Spearman agreement 78.1%. These are honest numbers for the report; instability mainly comes from weak guides.\n\nWait — one concern: did stage-2 flip anything else unexpectedly? Let me check the per_guide diagnostics: guide1→THOC5 (stage-1 is THOC5; not a complex member — no problem). guide10→CPSF6?? Previously (scratch variant) the greedy top for guide10 was SYMPK 0.199/RPRD1B 0.198; this run has CPSF6 0.1985 with margin 0.0014. Slight difference because F features are slightly different (this run recomputes fsn on solid genes with n>=20 — same as the variant… minor floating-point differences). OK.\n\nAlso note that guide32→PAPOLA (0.076) is garbage but honestly flagged (low_n, low score).\n\n4. Additional flips to confirm: guide8→CPSF2 retained (good). Let me check complex_refined flags: probably only guide14 flipped. Let me check diagnostics. Also, was the CPSF complex rule triggered for CPSF guides, and did the NNLS weight check preserve all of them?\n\nNext, for the report let me verify which genes are unmatched (genes that don't appear in the CSV): from the table: genes used: THOC5, CPSF7, CPSF2 (×3: guide3,8,21), CPSF1, PCF11 (×2: guide5,20), SCAF4, PAPOLA (×2: guide7,32), RBBP6, CPSF6 (×2: guide10,19), SCAF1 (×6: 11,12,15,16,23,26,27 — no wait, that's 7: guide11,12,15,16,23,26,27 = 7), NUDT21, LEO1, CPSF4 (×3: 17,22,29), PABPC1, PABPN1, SYMPK, RPRD1B (×2: 28,30), CSTF1. Count: 1+1+3+1+2+1+2+1+2+7+1+1+3+1+1+1+2+1 = 32 ✓. Unmatched reference genes (33-19 used): CPSF3, CPSF3L, CSTF2, CSTF3, CDC73, CTR9, FIP1L1, PAF1, PHF3, RPAP2, RPRD1A, SCAF8, SCAF11, SRSF3 — that's 14 genes unmatched. Hmm, quite a lot. This reflects that the greedy per-guide argmax concentrates on attractors. In the report I should present the gene-pull view as well (which guide is best for each reference gene) — this gives a more balanced picture (e.g., CPSF3 pull winner is guide4, SRSF3 is guide30, etc.).\n\nFor the report, let me pull key numbers from diagnostics.json, verify which guides got refined in stage-2, and check the unconstrained Hungarian assignment for context. One more quick script (cheap, just reading JSON + small computation). Actually the Hungarian assignment isn't in the diagnostics; I only saved the totals. Let me quickly compute the unconstrained Hungarian assignment and include it in the report as a sensitivity analysis (the report can include a one-to-one view). Small script, no problem.\n"}}
