{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"reasoning","text":"All verified:\n- Only guide14 was refined in Stage 2 (PAF1 → LEO1). All other guides retained their Stage 1 assignments.\n- All guides significant with p=0.005 except guide32 (p=0.065, n=3 — noise, flagged).\n- LEO1 disambiguation block complete: within-complex r (PAF1 leads), NNLS weights (LEO1 0.252 dominates), cell voting (PAF1 leads but mean r is ~0.1, weak), gene pull (guide14 is decisive: 0.335 vs runner-up 0.177), bijection totals (guide14=LEO1 matches the global optimum 13.366 > guide14=PAF1's 13.344).\n\nInteresting point: raw_profile_centroid_max_r mean 0.895 — the raw profile correlates ~0.9 with the reference centroid regardless of gene. That is, naive centroid mapping is dominated by dataset/cell-state similarity (all guides match every reference centroid at r≈0.9, with no discriminative power) — a good demonstration for the report. Actually, let me re-phrase: the control I computed was the max r of each query guide's raw profile against the reference gene raw centroids — mean 0.895, max 0.916: everything correlates highly with everything (because the cell-type baseline dominates), so argmax is driven by tiny differences in state rather than perturbation. The signature approach removes that baseline.\n\nNext, write report.md. Contents:\n1. Task and data overview\n2. Cell-type shift characterization (PC1 separation ~10.4 pooled SD; UMAP centroid; raw profile centroid control mean r 0.895, non-discriminative)\n3. Methods (NT reference signature, F-ratio features K=1500, Pearson, Stage-2 complex NNLS refinement; leakage-prevention procedure)\n4. Results: guide of interest table; full mapping table (from CSV); gene-pull note (unmatched genes)\n5. Quantification of ambiguity: margins (median, min), p-values, near-ties, low-n flags, subsample stability 81.2%, Spearman agreement 78.1%, attractor note (SCAF1 is taken by 7 guides), per-guide table\n6. LEO1 case study: all four lines of evidence, conclusion and confidence\n7. Validation and controls\n8. Limitations\n\nKeep it concise but complete. Include the mapping table. Also state deliverable paths and reproducibility (python output/analysis.py, seed).\n\nNote on numbers for the report:\n- PC1 separation: query mean 12.57±2.92, reference −10.57±1.15, separation 10.44 pooled SD (from exploratory analysis).\n- NT vs NT profile r = 0.9178.\n- Number of genes: common 11858; query 3516 cells (1424 NT), reference 4183 (922 NT).\n- Query guides 32 (n range 3–208), reference genes 33.\n- F-ratio features K=1500; MIN_REF_N_FOR_FSN=20 → 30 solid genes.\n- Median Stage-1 score 0.483; range 0.076–0.721.\n- All permutation p-values 0.005, except guide32 at 0.065.\n- Stability: final assignment under 80% subsampling 81.25% (unstable guides = weak ones; anchors stable).\n- Spearman Stage-1 agreement 78.1%.\n- Margins: PABPC1 0.2173, NUDT21 0.1029, LEO1 −0.0844 (negative → flag).\n- 14 reference genes not reached by greedy argmax (listed); the gene-pull view is given for completeness.\n\nWait, actually let me re-count unmatched genes from the final CSV: matched genes = {THOC5, CPSF7, CPSF2, CPSF1, PCF11, SCAF4, PAPOLA, RBBP6, CPSF6, SCAF1, NUDT21, LEO1, CPSF4, PABPC1, PABPN1, SYMPK, RPRD1B, CSTF1} = 18 genes. Unmatched = 33−18 = 15: CPSF3, CPSF3L, CSTF2, CSTF3, CDC73, CTR9, FIP1L1, PAF1, PHF3, RPAP2, RPRD1A, SCAF8, SCAF11, SRSF3 — that's 14. Let me count again: CPSF3, CPSF3L (2), CSTF2, CSTF3 (4), CDC73, CTR9 (6), FIP1L1 (7), PAF1 (8), PHF3 (9), RPAP2 (10), RPRD1A (11), SCAF8 (12), SCAF11 (13), SRSF3 (14). 18+14=32. Wait, 33−18=15. I'm missing one: SYMPK is matched. CDC73… hmm, matched list: THOC5, CPSF7, CPSF2, CPSF1, PCF11, SCAF4, PAPOLA, RBBP6, CPSF6, SCAF1, NUDT21, LEO1, CPSF4, PABPC1, PABPN1, SYMPK, RPRD1B, CSTF1 — that's 18. Total 33 → 15 unmatched: my enumeration above was 14; the missing one is… full reference list: CDC73, CPSF1, CPSF2, CPSF3, CPSF3L, CPSF4, CPSF6, CPSF7, CSTF1, CSTF2, CSTF3, CTR9, FIP1L1, LEO1, NUDT21, PABPC1, PABPN1, PAF1, PAPOLA, PCF11, PHF3, RBBP6, RPAP2, RPRD1A, RPRD1B, SCAF1, SCAF11, SCAF4, SCAF8, SF3A1, SRSF3, SYMPK, THOC5. SF3A1! I forgot SF3A1. So unmatched 15: CPSF3, CPSF3L, CSTF2, CSTF3, CTR9, CDC73, FIP1L1, PAF1, PHF3, RPAP2, RPRD1A, SCAF8, SCAF11, SF3A1, SRSF3.\n\nIn the report I'll present the top pull guide for each reference gene (computed from C) — I have some numbers from earlier scratch (pull table with F features): CPSF1 pull=0.725 guide4, NUDT21 0.708 guide13, CPSF4 0.707 guide4, CPSF3 0.678 guide4, PCF11 0.673 guide20, PABPC1 0.664 guide18, CPSF2 0.662 guide4, CSTF3 0.651 guide4, CSTF1 0.634 guide4, SYMPK 0.617 guide4, CPSF6 0.613 guide19, FIP1L1 0.568 guide20, RBBP6 0.562 guide9, SCAF1 0.555 guide16, PABPN1 0.532 guide24, PAPOLA 0.473 guide7, RPRD1B 0.473 guide30, PAF1 0.455 guide14, CTR9 0.440 guide14, CDC73 0.428 guide14, SCAF4 0.403 guide6, RPAP2 0.399 guide16, SRSF3 0.393 guide30, THOC5 0.375 guide20, SF3A1 0.362 guide20, LEO1 0.353 guide14, CSTF2 0.344 guide4, CPSF3L 0.319 guide30, CPSF7 0.293 guide2, RPRD1A 0.236 guide20, SCAF11 0.169 guide18, PHF3 0.141 guide1, SCAF8 0.132 guide30. (These are from the variant run — the numbers in the final run differ slightly, e.g., LEO1 pull guide14 0.3349 vs 0.353; I'll cite from the diagnostics where possible and mark these pull numbers as approximate — actually, to be accurate I should recompute the pull table with the final pipeline's C. Let me quickly add it — load the h5ad once more, or… more simply: a small script that re-runs the Pipeline class imported from output/analysis.py? Importing analysis.py re-runs the entire script (no main guard). Hmm — that means re-running the full pipeline (fast, ~8 seconds). I can extract it by running `python -c \"import runpy; ...\"` — more simply: write a small script that imports analysis.py as a module via exec and uses P.C… but that would duplicate all output.\n\nCleaner: add pull-table computation to analysis.py itself? The artifact set is already verified; per BOUNDED execution, don't do a full re-run for report-only changes. But small edits to analysis.py + one fast re-run (~30 seconds) is fine, and keeps analysis.py as the single source of truth. Actually — diagnostics.json already contains everything needed for the report. Except the full pull table. The report can include the pull table for just the 15 unmatched genes. Recompute with one small standalone script that imports the Pipeline from output/analysis.py… can't import without running.\n\nDecision: leave analysis.py as is (verified). For the pull table, run one small standalone script that minimally recomputes the signatures (5 seconds, already done in scratch_leo.py — there's an exact pull table from the same F-ratio pipeline, and the numbers differ from the final run in the third decimal place due to… actually scratch_leo used exactly the same recipe (F-ratio, K=1500, solid genes with n≥20) — the numbers should match the final C except for floating-point noise. But the final run's guide14 LEO1 = 0.3349 vs scratch_leo 0.3525 — hmm, that's not floating-point noise. Why? In scratch_leo, `solid` used the condition `max(rn[g] for guides of t) >= 20` — same… wait, in the final Pipeline, fsn uses the nt_grp of all NT groups (10) — scratch_leo also used all NT groups. Difference: scratch_leo computed f with S from solid genes… same. Hmm, 0.3525 vs 0.3349 — let me check: in scratch_leo, the feature selection was built from solid genes with rn >= 20, rn = guide-level n; in Pipeline, r_ns is guide-level counts, same. Difference: scratch_leo used K=1500 of argsort(f)[::-1], same…\n\nAh wait — got it: in the final Pipeline, `nt_grp` = per-NT-group signatures computed as rX[(rl==g)].mean(0) − r_nt for each NT group g — same as scratch. Hmm, but the final run's guide14 within-PAF r was PAF1=0.419 vs scratch_variant 0.455. Systematic shift. What's different? In scratch_variant/scratch_leo, feature-selection variance S.var(0) was computed on signatures of solid genes; same here…\n\nDifference: scratch_leo's signatures used `qX[ql==g].mean(0)-qnt`, and ql comes from q.obs[\"guide\"].astype(str).values loaded fresh; Pipeline is the same. Hmm — the densify dtype is float32 in both.\n\nActually — found it: in scratch_leo I used `common = q.var_names.intersection(r.var_names)` and then sliced; Pipeline: same. But gene order: intersection preserves q's order in both. Hmm.\n\nAnother difference: in scratch_leo, F-ratio denominator N.var(0), N = stacked NT-group signatures — same…\n\nWait, actually in the final Pipeline the nt_grp signatures are computed as `rX[(rl == g)].mean(axis=0) - self.r_nt for g in pd.unique(rl) if is_nt(g)` — pd.unique(rl) is in first-appearance order — same set as scratch. Same 10 groups.\n\nHmm, let me look at scratch_leo once more:\n```\nS = np.vstack([rs[t] for t in genes])   # ← all 33 genes, not solid!\nN = np.vstack([rX[(rl==g)].mean(0)-rnt for g in pd.unique(rl) if g.startswith(\"NT\")])\nf = S.var(0)/(N.var(0)+1e-8)\n```\nThat's it! scratch_leo used all 33 genes (including noisy SYMPK n=8, RPAP2 n=5) in S, whereas Pipeline uses only solid genes (n≥20, 30 genes) for the variance computation. This slightly changes feature selection → slightly different correlations. OK — the final Pipeline version is the canonical one. For the report's pull table I should use the final pipeline's C. One clean way: modify analysis.py to save the per-guide full correlation matrix to diagnostics? That bloats the JSON (32×33 floats = small, actually no problem). Or add a per-reference-gene pull summary to diagnostics: pull_gene table: for each reference gene: best guide, pull r, runner-up guide. Small, useful, add to analysis.py, re-run once (~30 seconds). This is justified (analysis.py becomes fully self-contained for the report). \n\nAlso for the report I'd like the PC1 separation stats — these came from exploratory scanpy runs, not from analysis.py. I can cite them as a characterization from exploratory analysis (with a note). That's fine — describe them as characterization in the report (reproducible with scanpy). Or quickly add to analysis.py… keep analysis.py focused; cite the characterization numbers in the report as exploratory (10.4 SD). OK.\n\nLet me patch analysis.py to add the pull summary + then write the report."}}
